entry.summary:
I have deployed code to: Bare metal servers that screamed when the fan failed. VPS machines that mysteriously rebooted at 3AM. Kubernetes clusters that required three YAML sacrifices and a Helm incantation. And of course, AWS — where the bill is the only truly consistent runtime. And after years of “cloud-native architecture,” I’ve realized something uncomfortable: The cloud is not your computer. When you write Go: err := db.QueryRowContext(ctx, query).Scan(&user.ID)
It feels deterministic. When you write Rust: let user = repo.find_user(id).await?;
It feels safe. Structured. Controlled. Owned. You think: I wrote this code. I understand this system. But in the cloud? Your “server” is virtual. Your “disk” is network-attached. Your “network” is software-defined. Your “security boundary” is …
